{
  "id": "pipeline-churn",
  "title": "A running pipeline instance survives repeated graph replacement and unload and reload with work in flight, and renders exactly its last loaded graph",
  "binding": "Both legs boot the pipeline-feedback graph offscreen and let it run. Three times over, the running instance replaces its graph twice (the corrected edit, then the feedback graph), each install retiring the replaced graph behind submissions still in flight, with a reset and three submissions after each; then its layout slot stops showing it, its views.pipelines row is removed, which releases the instance (a following pipeline.inspect is refused), and the row is loaded again as a new instance that compiles, installs and submits four frames. Run with puck canary --debug-layers, the validation layers must report nothing: any [vulkan-debug] validation line or any [d3d12-debug] line fails both legs; the loader's general notices about the machine's own layers do not. Finally the instance is paused and reset and steps to submission 2, which the positive leg's last reload, the feedback graph, renders as 2/16. Before the capture creates the readback's staging buffer the instance owns exactly its installed graph, 49152 bytes; both backends' readbacks keep that buffer for the next capture of the same extent, so after the capture it owns 53248 bytes, the graph plus exactly 32 x 32 x 4. The discriminating leg's last reload loads the corrected edit instead, so submission 2 reads 1 - 2/16 and the positive leg's pixel oracle turns red.",
  "bootShape": "offscreen",
  "backends": [
    "vulkan",
    "directx"
  ],
  "requirements": [
    "gpu"
  ],
  "timeoutSeconds": 60,
